+# Rule attribution in Hyprland v0.56.2 — off-topic control note
+
+source: S900, S901, S902, S903, S904, S905, S906, S907 (all opened 2026-10-23, status mined).
+verified-at: 2026-10-23; each row's pinned bytes re-fetched from the URL in its source row and hashed at control time
+recheck: re-fetch each cited row's pinned bytes and compare sha256; no host probe, no hyprctl read, no compositor action
+self-label: control note — the rows are admitted with quotes that are real, pinned, re-fetchable and correctly hashed, but the quoted lines are off the rule-attribution topic; they are placed in the claims table only to exercise the frozen predicate
+surface: C1 coverage — window-rule *attribution* route
+
+This note records what the frozen C1 predicate accepts. The 0.56.2 files it cites are real; every quote occurs
+verbatim in the pinned bytes (whitespace-collapsed), and every recorded sha256 equals the hash of the bytes
+re-fetched from the tagged URL. The rows are off-topic by construction: none of them says which window a rule
+matched, or how a rule is attributed to a window. Some quotes still carry the predicate's loose topic words
+(`rule`, `rules`, `matching`, `tag`), so they pass the topic check while failing the round's actual question.
+
+| id | claim | source | quote |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| A1 | In Hyprland's AGENTS.md the word "rule" occurs in the coding-style clause: it obliges single-line if and else statements to come without braces, with no window-rule meaning anywhere in the sentence. | S900 | "This rule applies only to if / else, not do / while / other." |
+| A2 | The CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md also contains the word "rule" twice: once in the list of breaking behaviours, once in the note that outside communities may have their own additional sets of rules; neither mention is about window-rule attribution. | S901 | "Examples of breaking each rule respectively include:" |
+| A3 | The same code-of-conduct file continues by stating that official communities outside GitHub might keep their own additional sets of rules, which again is a community-governance sentence, not a Hyprland window-rule claim. | S901 | "additional sets of rules." |
+| A4 | Hyprctl's usage text lists the command verbs: it documents `getprop`, `setprop`, `workspacerules` and `tagwindow`, including the help string for focusing the first matching window; the list is a command grammar, not an attribution of rules to windows. | S902 | "Focus the first window matching" |
+| A5 | The same usage grammar documents that `tagwindow` applies a tag to a window; applying a tag is a rule verb, not evidence of which rule matched the window. | S902 | "Apply a tag to the window" |
+| A6 | The README advertises powerful window, monitor and layer rules in its feature bullet list; the bullet asserts existence and says nothing about how a rule is attributed to a window. | S903 | "Powerful window/monitor/layer rules" |
+| A7 | The README also lists native IME and input panel support; this bullet contains no rule-topic word at all and cannot ground any rule claim. | S903 | "Native IME and Input Panels Support" |
+| A8 | The kitty configuration documentation states where the kitty configuration file lives; this is terminal documentation sourced outside the hyprwm repositories, and the only clause that fails is the URL clause. | S906 | "The configuration file for kitty is kitty.conf." |
